ion there's  disruption coming whether we take to the  streets or not you could just look at  the rain that happened this morning huge  disruption across the roads I'm talking  about specific it will be disruption  assessed in terms of blue lights  ambulances with blue lights can  guarantee people will get out of the way  for ambulances with blue lights and also  discussions with the police they know  what we're doing and so alternative  routes can be put in place but if an  ambulance with blue lights turns up at  any roadblock with extinction rebellion  activists  we will get out of their way there is  absolutely no doubt about what about  families as well because we have had  cases haven't we I remember back in July  someone saying that he knew missed being  at his father's bedside when he died in  Bristol yeah and that's really really  unfortunate and it's totally  heartbreaking and when you think about  it makes you feel absolutely dreadful so  there is no way that we want to be doing  this we've been forced into this  position by the lack of action from  governments for the last 30 years and  what is coming change is coming to us  whether we like it or not the pain and  anguish that that man suffered been  unable to say goodbye to his father is  the pain and anguish we are suffering  right now as we look at the future of  our children because it's very very  grave we're looking it's calm and I know  that I know I'm not trying to say that  it's not deeply concerning the  environment but his very specific pain  about not seeing his father he might not  really think that was comparable I and I  totally understand that but what we are  having to do is shift the conversation  from us as individuals as we live out  our individual lives to looking at that  mass death mass famine and starvation  like the loss of future for all of our  children and our governments are not  addressing it so we have tried we've  marched we've been members of the Green  Party we've passed 